    "created_at": "2017-07-07T20:43:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-07-07T20:44:06Z",
    "body": "> Is there such thing as a problem that we don't already have a solution for? A `<div>` with css and javascript can do pretty much everything (as the React community has shown)\r\n\r\nSure! For example, if there were no way to make a `<progress>` indeterminate, that would be a problem we don't have a solution for. Or if there were no way to turn an indeterminate progress bar into one that is not indeterminate.\r\n\r\n> Would making the DOM API more intuitive not be a good enough reason to update it?\r\n\r\nThese kind of ergonomics initiatives are rare. They require a proven track record in libraries (e.g. jQuery is a successful example of a library whose ergonomics were popular enough to eventually be incorporated). We need to show beyond a shadow of a doubt that the proposal is a significant enough benefit to developers, and something they are already adding themselves, that it is worth the cost to update the spec, write tests, get four implementations to ship it, and then wait for it to trickle down to enough users' browsers that web developers can use it reliably without including a polyfill library anyway.\r\n\r\nIn this case, you've given us no evidence that this is more intuitive. Indeed, I think we have a lot of evidence from the confusion over the checkbox API (you linked to one example, in the React community) that shows is less intuitive to have multiple properties control a single attribute. Right now we have a very intuitive and straightforward model where the `value` property mirrors the `value` attribute, and indeterminateness is one of the possible values.\r\n\r\nIf you truly believe this is better, I encourage you to create a library implementing it, and if it gets significant uptake from developers who find it easier to use, we can consider that evidence that we were wrong and we should change the standard.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
